// Shared Syncthing ignore patterns applied to every folder by apply-ignores.sh.
// Keep this list generic — no project, path, or host-specific entries.
// Per-folder overrides live in ~/.config/syncthing-sync/overrides.json (not versioned).

// macOS
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
.fseventsd
.TemporaryItems
.DocumentRevisions-V100
Icon?

// Editors / IDEs
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.idea
.vscode/.history

// Build / dependency directories
// Only patterns that are unambiguously tool-specific go here. Common English
// words like `build`, `dist`, `target` are intentionally NOT included — they
// can collide with user data directories named the same thing (e.g. a CNC
// project's `build/` containing photos). Add those as per-folder overrides
// in ~/.config/syncthing-sync/overrides.json for code-only folders.
node_modules
.venv
__pycache__
*.pyc
.next
.turbo
.cache
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
.mypy_cache

// VCS
//
// Git repositories are NO LONGER synced through Syncthing — that partial
// .git/ replication was the source of the sync-conflict pileups (500 files on
// 2026-07-07). Work-in-progress now flows via git itself:
// `sync-gh.py --wip-only` pushes a wip/<host> branch for private GitHub repos
// and writes a git bundle (~/.local/share/wip-bundles) for the rest; pick it
// up on another machine with the `wip` command. ~/Code is no longer a shared
// folder, so active repos aren't in Syncthing at all.
//
// Ignore any .git directory as defense-in-depth: if a stray repo ends up
// under a shared folder (e.g. ~/Projects), this stops its object/ref churn
// from reintroducing conflicts. Keep active repos under ~/Code/gh, not inside
// a Syncthing folder.
.git

// Atomic bundle writes land as <name>.bundle.tmp before an atomic rename;
// never sync the half-written temp file.
*.bundle.tmp
